// glossary.typ — alphabetised glossary of terms used in the guide (Task 8)
=== AI Fireman

An automatic in-game fireman that fires a steam locomotive's boiler for you, shown in the F5 Train Driving Info window alongside a manual-firing display.

=== Boiler pressure

The steam pressure in a steam locomotive's boiler, shown on the F5 window with a boiler heat input/output indicator.

=== Brake cylinder (BC)

The air-brake cylinder on a car or locomotive; its pressure is one of the two Brake Reservoir numbers shown in the F5 Train Driving Info window (alongside the equalization reservoir).

=== Brake flow

The rate of air flowing into the brake pipe during release and recharge, shown on the F5 window.

=== Brake pipe

The continuous air line running the length of a train; its pressure is reported twice on the F5 window — in the lead engine and in the last car — and the difference shows how fast the air system is charging down a long train.

=== Cab

The driver's seat — the compartment from which the engineer controls the locomotive, shown by pressing 1 in the cab view.

=== Consist

A set of coupled vehicles — locomotives, wagons and carriages — forming a train.

=== Content form

The built-in catalogue and installer that pops up when Open Rails first launches. Every route in this guide is installed through it.

=== Coupler

The mechanism that joins vehicles together. Coupling brings the couplers together and engages them; uncoupling releases them.

=== Dynamic brake

A locomotive braking method that turns the traction motors into generators to slow the train; notched controls, used on mountain downgrades, and only available when the throttle is fully closed. Controlled by the comma and period keys.

=== Equalization reservoir (EQ)

The brake-control reservoir in the locomotive; its pressure is one of the two Brake Reservoir numbers shown on the F5 window.

=== Graduated release

An air-brake setting that allows a partial release of the brakes (passenger standard); without it, braking behaves to freight standard and is fully released.

=== Handbrake

The parking brake on an individual car, used to hold a wagon in place once it has been uncoupled and left standing.

=== HUD

Head-up display — the on-screen overlay of driving information. In this guide the F5 Train Driving Info window is the HUD.

=== Immersive mode

A Track Monitor (F4) mode toggled with Shift+F4 that conceals upcoming signal aspects, speed limits and diverging switches while keeping signal, milepost, siding and station locations.

=== Industry spotting

Placing a car precisely at an industry — such as the Craven Timber Railway's sawmill — so it can be loaded or unloaded.

=== Install Path

The folder, set or confirmed in the Content form, where content is installed.

=== Limited

A named premium passenger service. Wave 3's "The Limited" is a named steam service running over the Blue Mountains.

=== Manual firing

Driving a steam locomotive with the boiler fired by the player rather than the AI Fireman; the F5 window shows a manual-firing display.

=== Notch

A fixed step position of a diesel or electric locomotive's throttle; some steps are "smooth" and adjust power gradually.

=== Pathless train

A train without an assigned path, driven manually (e.g. a static consist taken over from the Train List window) in which you can move switches, change direction and couple/uncouple.

=== Regulator

The continuous throttle of a steam locomotive, which can be set to any opening smoothly rather than in notches.

=== Runaround

Moving the locomotive around its train — pulling past it, uncoupling, and coupling onto the other end — so it can push from behind.

=== Saddle tank

A small steam locomotive whose water tank straddles the boiler. The Craven line's PWD32 and 529X are saddle tanks.

=== Signal

An aspect that governs movement, telling the engineer to proceed, to be prepared to stop at the next signal, or to stop right there.

=== SPAD

Signal passed at danger; if the train goes out of control after a SPAD, press Ctrl+M to exit emergency braking.

=== Superelevation

The banking of the track on curves, which lets trains hold higher speeds through them. Open Rails simulates it as part of the default simulation.

=== Throttle / regulator

The control admitting power to the wheels on a diesel, or steam to the cylinders on a steam locomotive. The regulator is the steam-engine name for the same control.

=== Timetable

The scheduled running plan a train is meant to follow. The Limited runs to a timetable; the yard does not.

=== Track monitor

The F4 window showing track information, signals and the speed limit.

=== Train brake

The brake controlling the whole train, applied from the cab, as opposed to a single car's handbrake.

=== Train Operations Monitor

The Ctrl+Alt+F9 window for controlling individual cars: air brake hose connections, uncoupling with or without air pressure, handbrakes, and releasing air so a car can coast.

=== Yard / switching

Low-speed car movement operations — coupling, uncoupling, shunting, runaround moves and industry spotting.

=== Zig-zag

A track alignment that reverses direction to climb, running forward on one ledge, reversing on a switchback, and running forward again on the next. The Great Zig Zag Railway's signature move.
